---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="YouTube money calculator: what views are worth in ad revenue"
  description="Estimate YouTube ad earnings from monthly views and your niche. Uses published creator RPM figures by niche, and says plainly why every estimate like this is a range."
>
  <h1>YouTube money calculator</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    YouTube pays creators a share of the advertising money, worked out per thousand views.
    Put in your monthly views, pick the kind of channel, and this page gives an honest range,
    not a single flattering number.
  </p>
    <figure class="fig fig--creator"><img src="/img/youtube.webp" alt="A video editing timeline on a computer screen" width="1200" height="514" decoding="async"></figure>

  <div class="sheet">
    <div class="field-grid">
      <div>
        <label for="views">Views per month</label>
        <input type="number" id="views" inputmode="numeric" min="0" step="any" value="100000" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="niche">Channel kind</label>
        <select id="niche">
          <option value="10.96">Finance and investing</option>
          <option value="8.44">Business and marketing</option>
          <option value="5.50" selected>Tech and reviews</option>
          <option value="4.45">Lifestyle and vlogs</option>
          <option value="3.50">Gaming</option>
          <option value="4.00">Education and how-to</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for="kind">Video kind</label>
        <select id="kind">
          <option value="long" selected>Long videos</option>
          <option value="shorts">Shorts</option>
        </select>
      </div>
    </div>
    <button class="btn btn--primary" id="calc" type="button">Estimate the earnings</button>
    <div id="out" aria-live="polite" style="margin-top:1.5rem;"></div>
    <p class="result-note">
      An estimate, not a promise. Two channels with the same views can earn three times apart
      depending on where viewers live, how long they watch, and the month of the year. December
      advertising pays far more than January.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>How YouTube pay works</h2>
  <p>
    Advertisers pay to appear around videos. YouTube keeps 45% of that and the creator gets
    55%. What a creator actually receives per thousand views is called RPM, revenue per mille,
    and it is the only figure that matters here: earnings = views ÷ 1,000 × RPM. RPM already
    has YouTube’s cut taken out, which is why this page does not take another cut off the top.
  </p>
  <p>
    The niche figures in the dropdown are medians that creators have disclosed publicly,
    gathered and published as open data: finance channels at the top because advertisers pay
    well to reach people thinking about money, gaming and entertainment lower because the
    audience is young and spread across low paying countries. Shorts are a different world:
    the revenue pool is shared across all Shorts views, and creators typically see $0.03 to
    $0.07 per thousand, which is why the Shorts setting ignores the niche.
  </p>

  <h2>One example, worked by hand</h2>
  <p>
    A tech channel with 100,000 views a month at the tech median RPM of $5.50: 100,000 ÷ 1,000
    × 5.50 = <strong>$550 a month</strong>, $6,600 a year if it held steady, which it will
    not. The same views on a finance channel at $10.96 would be $1,096. On Shorts at $0.05,
    they would be $5. Views are not money until you know which views they are.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    <dt>How many views do I need to earn $1,000 a month?</dt>
    <dd>
      At the tech median, about 180,000 views a month. At the finance median, about 90,000. At
      the lifestyle median, about 225,000. Work it backwards for your own channel with the
      formula above: views needed = 1,000 × target ÷ your RPM.
    </dd>
    <dt>When does YouTube start paying at all?</dt>
    <dd>
      After you are accepted into the Partner Program and pass a $100 payment threshold.
      Note the announced change: from 1 February 2027, new applicants need 8,000 qualified
      watch hours in a year, or 20 million qualified Shorts views in 90 days, double the
      figures before that date. Check YouTube’s own help pages for the current rule, because
      it is their number to change.
    </dd>
    <dt>Is ad revenue the whole income?</dt>
    <dd>
      For most working creators, no. Sponsorships, affiliate links, memberships and their own
      products often outweigh ads. This calculator only counts the ad share, because that is
      the part views alone can estimate.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p class="meta">Method: views ÷ 1,000 × RPM, niche medians from creator-disclosed figures published as open data (CC BY 4.0). Page last checked 13 November 2026.</p>

  <script>
    const $ = (id) => document.getElementById(id);
    const out = $('out');
    const money = (n) => '$' + n.toLocaleString('en-GB', { maximumFractionDigits: 0 });

    function compute() {
      const views = parseFloat($('views').value) || 0;
      const shorts = $('kind').value === 'shorts';
      const rpm = shorts ? 0.05 : parseFloat($('niche').value);
      const monthly = views / 1000 * rpm;
      const low = monthly * 0.6;
      const high = monthly * 1.6;
      out.innerHTML =
        '<p class="result-figure">About ' + money(monthly) + ' a month</p>' +
        '<p>Realistically somewhere between ' + money(low) + ' and ' + money(high) + ' a month, depending on where your viewers live and the time of year. That is about ' + money(monthly * 12) + ' a year at the middle figure.</p>' +
        (shorts ? '<p class="result-note">Shorts use a flat $0.05 per thousand views, the middle of the $0.03 to $0.07 range creators report.</p>' : '');
    }

    $('calc').addEventListener('click', compute);
    compute();
  </script>
</Base>
